editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Sort pairs lexicographically. All pairs with value x become consecutive, ordered by increasing original position. Since no position exceeds n, upper_bound on (x,n) skips the entire group. If the predecessor exists and has value x, its position is the answer. Otherwise x is absent.

## Walk through the sample
The three records for value 8 retain original positions 1, 3 and 6. Searching past the entire equal-value group lands just after (8,6). Returning its original position gives 6 even though sorting moved the records.

## Complexity
Sorting costs O(n log n). The t queries take O(t log n) altogether. Store O(n) pairs and process queries as they arrive.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, t;
    cin >> n >> t;
    vector<pair<int, int>> a(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        cin >> a[i].first;
        a[i].second = i + 1;
    }
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    while (t--) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        auto it = upper_bound(a.begin(), a.end(), pair{x, n});
        cout << (it != a.begin() && prev(it)->first == x ? prev(it)->second : -1) << '\n';
    }
}
~~~
